msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Master password"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2493
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Your items, Secret Key and recovery parts stay the same."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2494
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Changing it re-checks your current master password against the vault file, "
"so someone using a computer you left unlocked can't do it."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2498
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Change master password…"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2508
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Secret Key on this computer"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2510
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Kept under a key in this computer's security chip (TPM), which never leaves "
"it. A copy of this computer's disk or of your Windows profile doesn't carry "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2511
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Kept by Windows alone: this computer has no security chip (TPM) the app can "
"use."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2512
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Your Secret Key and your master password together open the vault, so this "
"computer keeps the Secret Key for you. A program running as you can still "
"ask for it while you're signed in, as it could ask Windows; the chip stops "
"it from being copied away and opened on another computer. If the chip is "
"ever cleared or replaced, the vault asks once for the Secret Key from your "
"emergency kit."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2514
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Weaker than with a chip: a copy of your Windows profile, with your Windows "
"password, opens it on another computer."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2523
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Item lock"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2524
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"A second check before an open vault shows passwords, codes and hidden "
"fields. Names, usernames and websites stay visible so you can find the "
"account."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2525
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"With a PIN or another password, the locked secrets are also encrypted with a "
"key that only that PIN or password opens: a program that reads the open "
"vault from your computer's memory still can't read items that are locked. A "
"4-digit PIN has only 10,000 combinations, and such a program could try them "
"all in a few hours, so use 6 to 8 digits or a password for real strength. "
"With the master password as the check nothing extra is encrypted; it only "
"stops someone at your unlocked computer. One right answer opens every locked "
"item for 5 minutes, or until you click Lock. After 5 wrong tries the whole "
"vault locks. A forgotten PIN is replaced here, with your master password."
msgstr ""

